-The eviction policy is still open. Candidates are LRU, frequency-based eviction with aging, and 2Q. LRU has little bookkeeping and follows recent work quickly but is vulnerable to sequential scans. Aged frequency counts protect repeatedly popular clips but add counter maintenance and can lag a project switch. 2Q separates first-use entries from reused entries, resisting scans at the cost of extra queues and a queue-size parameter. None changes the external interface, queue concurrency, or disk budget.
+## Eviction Policy (2Q)
+
+Eviction uses the 2Q policy, which partitions cached sheets by how often they are requested. Long sequential screenings produce many one-use sheets; comparison sessions repeatedly revisit a small working set. 2Q shields the working set from scan-driven eviction at the cost of two queues and a ghost list, all private to this worker.
+
+The policy maintains three structures over cache entries:
+
+- **A1-in** — a FIFO queue holding sheets admitted on first appearance, bounded to 3 GiB of sheet bytes (25% of the 12 GiB budget). Entries here are presumed one-off until proven otherwise.
+- **Am** — an LRU queue holding sheets that have been requested at least twice, bounded to the remaining cache budget. Every hit moves an entry to the front.
+- **A1-out (ghost list)** — a FIFO list of cache keys only, no JPEG bytes, capped at 2048 entries. When a sheet is evicted from A1-in without ever being promoted, its key enters the ghost list so a later re-request can be recognized as a reuse.
+
+**Admission.** When a completed sheet is admitted, its key is checked against the ghost list. A key absent from the ghost list enters A1-in; a key present in the ghost list has already survived one eviction cycle and is admitted directly into Am, with its ghost entry removed.
+
+**Promotion.** A hit on a sheet in A1-in promotes it to Am, moving its byte accounting to the Am bound. A hit on a sheet in Am moves it to the Am front. Ghost-list hits carry no bytes, so promoting from a ghost hit admits the sheet into Am and re-frees the bytes the ghost entry pretends to hold — the ghost entry is deleted at that point.
+
+**Eviction.** To free bytes before admission: evict from the tail of A1-in while A1-in's byte total exceeds its 3 GiB ceiling; then, if more space is still needed, evict the LRU tail of Am. Evicting from A1-in inserts the key into the ghost list; evicting from Am does not. Eviction never removes a sheet whose render job is still pending completion.
+
+## Decisions & Alternatives
+
+| Decision | Chosen | Alternatives Considered | Rationale |
+|----------|--------|------------------------|-----------|
+| Eviction policy | 2Q (A1-in FIFO + A1-out ghost list + Am LRU) | LRU; frequency counting with aging | Comparison sessions repeatedly revisit a small working set while screenings run long sequential scans; 2Q protects the reused sheets from scan eviction. LRU evicts hot comparison sheets during long screenings. Aged frequency counts add counter maintenance and retain yesterday's favorites too long after a project switch. All options leave the HTTP contract, render queue, and disk budget untouched. |
+| Ghost list capacity | 2048 keys, byteless | No ghost tracking; per-entry recency bit | Deep enough to span a full screening run's one-off keys; keys only, so the memory cost is negligible and eviction bookkeeping stays off the hot hit path. |
+| A1-in byte ceiling | 3 GiB (25% of the 12 GiB budget) | Larger or smaller A1-in splits (12.5%–50%) | Standard 2Q proportion; tuning deferred until pilot telemetry shows the real scan/reuse mix. |
+
+## Open Questions & Future Decisions
+
+### Resolved
+1. Eviction policy is 2Q, chosen over LRU and aged frequency counting to protect comparison-sheet reuse during sequential screenings.
+
+### Deferred
+1. Tune the A1-in byte ceiling and ghost-list capacity against pilot workload telemetry; both are constants in the design and need no interface change to adjust.
